BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF MELAWI,
a. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security from all forms of violence;
b. that all forms of acts of violence against women constitute violations of human rights, therefore their dignity and honor need to be protected, and their right to life is guaranteed in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
c. that violence against women continues to increase, therefore protection efforts are required;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection of Women from Acts of Violence;

Considering

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law...
4. Law Number 34 of 2003 concerning the Establishment of Melawi Regency and Sekadau Regency in the Province of West Kalimantan (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 149, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4344);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4976);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as last amended by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5659);
10. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);
11. Presidential Regulation Number 18 of 2014 concerning Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 44);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 01 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards (SPM) for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);
13. Regulation...
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 446);

With the Joint Approval of the REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MELAWI REGENCY
And

BAB II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The Implementation of Protection of Women victims of violence is based on:
a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment

Article 3

The Implementation of Protection of Women victims of violence aims to:
a. prevent violence against women;
b. provide services/handling to victims; and
c. empower women victims of violence and vulnerable groups;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women.

BAB III

SCOPE OF PROTECTION

Article 4

The Scope of Implementation of Protection of Women victims of violence includes:

a. prevention;
b. service/handling;
c. empowerment;

Article 5

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the SKPD in charge of Women's Empowerment;
2. Prevention Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by:
a. forming a working network;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a violence prevention system;
d. disseminating laws and regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence;

e. providing education for women victims of violence;
f. through Communication, Information and Education (KIE) media.

Article 8

Forms of service/handling of victims include:

a. complaint handling;
b. health/medical rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. law enforcement and legal aid services; and repatriation and social reintegration services;

Article 9

The Implementation of Service/handling of victims is carried out with:

a. fast and precise;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. free of charge; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality;

CHAPTER IV

VIOLENCE

Article 14

Violence against women consists of:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Article 17...

Article 17

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 14 letter c includes:

a. acts that are unnatural or unwanted;
b. coercion of sexual harassment;
c. coercion of sexual relations with and/or others for commercial purposes; coercion of sexual relations with other persons and/or for certain purposes.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 21

Every woman victim of violence has the right:

a. to have her dignity respected as a human being;
b. to obtain physical and psychological health recovery as well as social recovery according to the suffering experienced by the victim of violence;
c. to obtain...

d. to obtain protection from family, society, the Regional Government and/or other parties, either temporarily or based on a court determination/order;
e. special handling with the confidentiality of the victim;
f. accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of legislation;
g. spiritual guidance services;
h. to determine their own decisions.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government is obligated to implement and provide and is responsible for protection efforts for women victims of violence in the form of:

a. establishing PPT;
b. policy formulation;
c. planning and implementing programs and activities;
d. providing facilities and infrastructure support; and
e. supervising the implementation of services to victims with minimum service standards.

2. In implementing the obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government prepares a Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women.

3. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Governor Regulation.

Article 24

Society and family have the obligation and responsibility to:

a. prevent acts of violence against women;
b. provide information and/or report acts of violence against women to the competent authorities;
c. provide protection to victims;
d. provide emergency assistance;
e. participate in handling victims of violence.

CHAPTER X

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 29

( 1) The community participates in the implementation of protection by:
a. forming family partners at the community level;
b. forming community-based women's protection units;
c. independently conducting socialization of women's rights;
d. undertaking preventive measures if indications of violence against women are found in the community;
e. providing first aid to victims; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against women occurs in their environment.

3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and/or mass media.

CHAPTER XII

CLOSING PROVISIONS

Article 31

This Regional Regulation shall come into force on the date of promulgation.

In order that everyone may know of it, the promulgation of this Regional Regulation is ordered by its placement in the Regional Gazette of Melawi Regency.
